# /etc/default/earlyoom — userspace OOM killer config (#175)
#
# Why this is needed at all: exe.dev session processes inherit oom_score_adj
# -1000 from exe.dev's sshd (/exe.dev/bin/sshd), so the kernel OOM killer can
# NEVER pick the agent
# session or anything it launches. Under real exhaustion it takes the host's
# production service instead. earlyoom acts *before* the kernel does and can
# be told what to prefer, which the kernel killer cannot.
#
# BUT earlyoom cannot kill -1000 either (#178): v1.7 kill.c drops any
# oom_score_adj == -1000 process from candidacy AFTER applying the --prefer
# bonus. So the session's node/npm/npx never get picked, whatever --prefer
# says. What earlyoom actually chooses among is everything at adj > -1000.
# Measured 2026-09-23, with --prefer inert, the ranking was: (sd-pam) 733,
# llama-server 704, qdrant 685, then Postgres backends/checkpointer ~670.
#
# -r 3600     report memory state hourly (journal), not every 60s
# -m 6,3      warn at 6% free RAM, kill at 3%. No -s: this host has no swap.
# --avoid     never pick the web app, sshd, PID-1-ish supervisors, Postgres,
#             or (sd-pam). Postgres: SIGKILL on any backend makes the
#             postmaster reset every connection, which takes the web app down
#             by proxy. (sd-pam): tops the ranking at ~3.6 MB, so killing it
#             frees nothing and costs a kill cycle. '.sd-pam' stands in for
#             '\(sd-pam\)' — see NB3.
# --prefer    SocratiCode's containers (llama-server/ollama, qdrant): adj 0,
#             so actually killable, the largest transient RSS measured
#             (424 M + 213 M), and restarted on demand by ollama and by
#             Docker (restart=unless-stopped). node|npm|npx stay listed for
#             any caller not at -1000; for agent sessions they are inert.
#
# NB earlyoom 1.7 adds 300 to a --prefer match's score, so a preferred
# process outranks wslcb-web.service only while the service sits BELOW 300;
# its OOMScoreAdjust=-700 puts it at ~208. Changing one without the other
# silently re-arms the bug.
#
# NB2 these match /proc/<pid>/comm, which the kernel TRUNCATES to 15 chars —
# the npm launch shows up as 'npm exec socrat', not 'npm'. Do not anchor these
# with a trailing '$': it makes --prefer match nothing at all, and lets
# sshd-session and systemd-journal slip past --avoid. Verified against
# `ps -eo comm=` on this host.
#
# NB3 systemd, not a shell, parses this file: it strips the single quotes and
# passes each regex through as one argv entry (verified in
# /proc/<earlyoom>/cmdline). Keep the regexes free of backslashes; match
# literal punctuation with '.' instead of an escape.
# VALIDATED against earlyoom v1.7 on this host, 2026-09-19: the daemon echoed
# back both regexes and the 6%/3% thresholds. Re-check after any edit — a
# malformed EARLYOOM_ARGS fails at start, during an incident, on the one
# mechanism meant to handle it.
#
# `apt install earlyoom` starts the daemon BEFORE this file is in place, and
# `systemctl enable --now` will not restart an already-active unit — so the
# stock `-r 3600` (no regexes, 10%/5%) stays loaded while the unit reports
# active and enabled. Always restart, then read the journal, never infer from
# unit state:
#   sudo systemctl restart earlyoom
#   journalctl -u earlyoom -n 12 --no-pager   # must print BOTH regex lines
#                                             # and 6.00% / 3.00%
EARLYOOM_ARGS="-r 3600 -m 6,3 --avoid '^(uvicorn|sshd|systemd|exe-init|postgres|.sd-pam)' --prefer '^(node|npm|npx|llama-server|ollama|qdrant)'"
